{#
  view/j-translate/j-translate/edit.phtml — one phrase in every language, and what
  writing to it has replaced before.

  ## The form is rendered field by field, and the order is the data's

  `form_row(form.get('phrase'))` first, then one row plus one hidden id per locale, then
  the CSRF element and the submit. The per-locale pair is the part that cannot become a
  generic loop over the form's elements: each language's `<locale>Id` is the
  `trans_translations` row id being updated, and it travels as a hidden field beside the
  textarea it belongs to. Rendering the elements in declaration order would separate them.

  `phraseId` is hidden and is checked against the URL by the controller — it is what stops
  an edit of one phrase writing to another.

  ## The history panel

  Under the form and not beside it, because it is what you read *before* deciding, and the
  decision is the form above. Absent entirely when nothing has ever been overwritten, which
  is the common case — a phrase whose languages were only ever filled in has no thread, and
  an empty panel saying so on every screen would be noise.

  `previous` is a real recovery path: `trans_translations` keeps no copy, so before that
  table existed the replaced text was simply gone. Copying one back into the field above and
  saving is how a bad edit gets undone.

  Three shapes of row, and telling them apart is the whole point of the panel:

  - a **retirement** or **unretirement** is an event about the phrase, not a lost
    translation. It has no language and no previous text, and rendering it in the same shape
    as the rest would show two empty cells and imply something was destroyed. It answers
    "why did this leave the worklist", which is the question a reader most often arrives
    with. `unretire` is the same event in the other direction, and worth showing because a
    phrase that came back *because a page rendered it* writes no history at all — so a
    thread ending in a retirement means the site disagreed with whoever retired it.
  - a **retraction** loses the same text as an overwrite but leaves the language empty
    rather than replaced, so only one of the two is a translation somebody can look at on
    the site right now. Hence the `withdrawn` label.
  - anything else is an overwrite.

  The operation strings come from the model rather than being spelled here, so a rename in
  `TranslationsTable` cannot leave this template quietly matching nothing.
#}
{% extends jtranslate_layout %}

{% block content %}
<h1>{{ translate(page_title) }}</h1>
{{ form_open(form, form_action) }}
{{ form_hidden(form.get('phraseId')) }}
{{ form_row(form.get('phrase')) }}
{% for locale, locale_name in locales %}
{{ form_row(form.get(locale)) }}
{{ form_hidden(form.get(locale ~ 'Id')) }}
{% endfor %}
{{ form_row(form.get('security')) }}
{{ form_row(form.get('submit')) }}
{{ form_close() }}
{% if history is not empty %}
<hr>
<h2>{{ translate('Previous versions') }}</h2>
<p class="help-block">{{ translate('What writing to this phrase has replaced, newest first. Nothing here is rendered by the site any more — it is kept so a change can be reversed, and so the reason for a change survives the person who made it.') }}</p>
<table class="table responsive-table">
<thead><tr>
    <th>{{ translate('Language') }}</th>
    <th>{{ translate('Previous text') }}</th>
    <th>{{ translate('Note') }}</th>
    <th>{{ translate('Replaced') }}</th>
</tr></thead>
<tbody>
{% for entry in history %}
{% set operation = entry.operation|default(null) %}
{% set went_back = operation == retire_undone %}
{% if operation == retired or went_back %}
<tr class="text-muted">
    <td colspan="2"><span class="glyphicon glyphicon-{{ went_back ? 'share-alt' : 'inbox' }}"></span>&nbsp;{{ went_back ? translate('Put back on the worklist') : translate('Taken off the worklist') }}</td>
    <td>{% if entry.notes|default(null) is null or entry.notes == '' %}<span class="text-muted">&mdash;</span>{% else %}{{ entry.notes }}{% endif %}</td>
    <td>{% if entry.replaced_on|default(null) %}{{ entry.replaced_on }}{% endif %}{% if entry.replacedBy|default(null) is not null %}<br><small class="text-muted">{{ entry.replacedBy }}</small>{% endif %}</td>
</tr>
{% else %}
<tr>
    <td>{{ entry.locale|default('') }}{% if operation == retracted %}<br><span class="label label-warning">{{ translate('withdrawn') }}</span>{% endif %}</td>
    <td>{{ entry.old_translation|default('') }}</td>
    <td>{% if entry.notes|default(null) is null or entry.notes == '' %}<span class="text-muted">&mdash;</span>{% else %}{{ entry.notes }}{% endif %}</td>
    <td>{% if entry.replaced_on|default(null) %}{{ entry.replaced_on }}{% endif %}{% if entry.replacedBy|default(null) is not null %}<br><small class="text-muted">{{ entry.replacedBy }}</small>{% endif %}
    {#- The text domain is only worth naming when it is not this phrase's own: the thread is
        keyed on the phrase rather than the row, so it can carry entries written while the
        same string lived in another domain. -#}
    {%- if entry.text_domain|default(null) is not null and entry.text_domain != phrase.textDomain %}<br><small class="text-muted">{{ entry.text_domain }}</small>{% endif %}</td>
</tr>
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
</tbody>
</table>
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
